 and we delete them all we  don't want them around because we don't  we don't want overlap  we then  take our data frame of input and output  files and we map over that see the  pattern equals map that allows us to  iterate over one row at a time of this  data frame  to call our function remove points on  each one individually  you can see here that this function  depends upon  our nyc map our file name mapping and  in curly braces we included remove  filtered files because even though our  function doesn't make use of it i want  this step to only occur after that  removing data step has been successful  looking at the dag now we see that  removing the old files making the input  output list and the nyc map all feed  into filter files  now filtered files is a square not a  circle  because rather than one step it is a  pattern of steps because it's going to  be called for each of those rows in the  data frame so it's iterating over that  pattern  with our data all processed we can now  do our aggregation based filtering  this step uses tar change because our  function which calls arrow doesn't  actually refer to any of the previous  steps so i'm saying change equals the  filter file step so if those files  change  go ahead and run the step  the outcome this of this step will be a  file so i say format equals file  and i tell it to load up the package  arrow because this way i don't need to  specify the arrow namespace in that  function  targets and arrow don't really play  nicely together so you need to create  and destroy the arrow object within a  single target  and this gets even harder if you want to  share schemas  our dag now shows the process files  which will only run if the filter files  have changed  next up we need to get the docker  container running  so i create a step called docker up and  i actually have a list our step is a  list it has two parts to it  because your steps can be  any valid r object  the first step calls docker compose like  we saw ear